Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

> Benjamin Lewis wrote:
>> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
>
>> They already *are* two distinct things by default; the NetHack wizard
>> account is called "wizard", and the "unix wizard account" is called
>> "root".  (Unfortunately, the Debian Linux distribution has chosen to
>> make root the NetHack wizard account as well, instead of something more
>> reasonable such as "games".  This, however, is an issue with Debian
>> rather than NetHack.)
>
> Well, let's just say "things have changed", or at least I remember it
> so. "Back in the day", when I was a junior Unix sysadmin, one of my
> accounts was user "wizard", and my responsibilities with that account
>
>   (I _also_ had the password for account "root",
>   but shared it with Tad Guy, the senior sysadmin)
>
> were to maintain /usr/games.

This may have been true for your particular system, but I'm fairly certain
this was never a standard account name in any unix.

> If Linux now has that muddled with "root", all the worse; once upon a
> time, they were quite separate, and it is from that day that the NetHack
> user name "wizard" derives.

I'm not sure what you're talking about here.

> Unfortunately, I cannot confirm that memory independently; "wizard
> account" has become a generic term for the same thing, without the
> implication that the _name_ given to that account is actually
> "wizard". 

I've never heard of this generic term "wizard account" outside of NetHack,
and Google turns up little or nothing of relevance to unix.

> "User wizard" now also has another meaning, a tool for adding new users,
> and indeed the term "wizard" has broadened to the point that a google
> search overflows with results without being useful.

This is true for pretty much any word in the dictionary.

> The best evidence that "/usr/games" once belonged to user account
> "wizard" is the usage in NetHack itself, but that's entirely circular
> logic.

Indeed; and to the best of my knowledge it's not true.

> So, let's just say, in my fallible memory of 1985 events, user "wizard"
> owned the BSD Unix /usr/games directory, and I'll otherwise withdraw from
> the fray, except I _still_ think "wizard mode" needs splitting as
> previously described.

It *is* split.  When you compile NetHack, you can choose any name you like
for the "NetHack administrator" account.  This defaults to "wizard", a name
that does not exist by default on unix systems, unless a particular user
has chosen it as his or her login name.

Looking at Debian bug reports, I see that they changed the nethack admin
account to "root" after people complained that they didn't want to create a
new account in order to run wizmode.  I think that this was an entirely
wrong decision, and that they should have used user "games" instead, which
already exists on Debian.

